.\" ai-tools(1) -- project-lifecycle CLI of Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" Kept in sync with the CLI's usage() text by tests/unit/man.sh, which
.\" compares the long-option sets of both; @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted
.\" at deploy time (install.sh install_subst / the RPM %prep), like the CLI.
.TH AI-TOOLS 1 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
ai-tools \- manage the projects a sandboxed coding agent may work in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ai-tools
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ai-tools
registers, secures, and hands back the projects the sandboxed coding agent
(Claude Code, running as the unprivileged
.B ai-tools
system account) may work in. Run it as your own login user \(em never as the
sandbox account, which the CLI refuses for every command. Steps that need root
(secret lockdown, ownership, SELinux labels) invoke
.B sudo
themselves and prompt for your password.
.PP
Root may run the commands that write no operator-owned state \(em
.BR \-\-audit ,
.BR \-\-status ,
.BR \-\-list ,
.BR \-\-providers ,
.BR \-\-stop .
Every other command writes registries that belong to an operator and refuses
root, as does
.B \-\-for
in any combination.
.B \-\-audit
needs root either way, since the trail it reads is
.BR "700 root:root" ,
and
.B \-\-list
run by root reports root's own registry, which is empty on a normal host.
.B \-\-stop
is the one member of that set that acts rather than reports: its helper needs root either
way, and root is what an unattended detector usually runs as.
.PP
A command whose root helper your account holds no
.BR sudo (8)
rule for is refused before any password prompt, naming the command an operator
who does hold one can run instead \(em for most commands that is the same
command with
.BR \-\-for .
.PP
Every
.I path
argument defaults to the current working directory. Every yes/no question
states its default in the prompt
.RB ( "[Y/n] (default: Yes)" );
plain Enter \(em and any run without a terminal \(em takes the default, which
always falls on the safe side of the question.
.SS Operators and the sudo grant
Being an operator is two facts, both written by
.BR "ai\-tools\-admin operators add" :
membership of the
.B ai\-ops
group, and a name in
.B OPERATORS
in
.BR operator.conf (5).
A general
.BR sudo (8)
grant is neither of them \(em the host's own sudoers decides it, and no file in
this package writes or records it.
.PP
An operator holding only the two launches agent sessions and reads the reports.
The commands that write a registry \(em
.BR \-\-project\-claim ,
.BR \-\-project\-unclaim ,
.BR \-\-lockdown ,
.BR \-\-reclaim ,
.B \-\-sandbox\-create
\(em reach root helpers with no NOPASSWD rule and need the grant as well; an
operator who holds one performs them on another's behalf with
.BR \-\-for .
A host needs at least one operator holding the grant, since root is refused
every command that writes.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B \-\-project\-claim \fR[\fB\-y\fR] [\fIpath\fR]
Claim a real project in place so the agent works your actual tree: register it
(allowlist and git safe.directory), scan for and lock down secret-named files
.I before
any access is granted, set the shared group and ACL, and apply the SELinux
project label. Idempotent \(em a re-claim runs only the missing steps and
repairs interior permission drift behind the same confirm. Access to the
repository's git history
.RB ( .git )
is a separate opt-in prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-project\-create \fIpath\fR
Create a
.I new
project directory and claim it: one
.BR mkdir ,
an empty git repository, a
.B README.md
carrying the directory's own name, then the ordinary claim flow on the result.
The
.I path
is required and must
.I not
exist \(em claiming a directory that already exists is
.BR \-\-project\-claim ,
and keeping the two apart is what stops the operation that grants an agent access
to an existing tree from being reachable by a typo. Its parent
.I must
exist: only the final component is created, so a mistyped path is refused rather
than manufactured. Every filesystem step runs as the operator the run acts for, so
a create under
.B \-\-for
produces a tree that operator owns. A refusal removes the directory it made.
.IP
It does not take options, and asks for no confirmation of its own: the tree it makes is empty,
so the claim that follows infers the answers its prompts would otherwise ask for \(em
there are no pre-existing permissions to warn about, no secret-named files to scan a
brand-new directory for, and no git history to expose in a repository with no commits.
The one question that can still appear is the traverse grant on a parent directory,
which widens access above the project and is answered only by a person at a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-project\-unclaim \fR[\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-full\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\-entry\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-\-group\fI g\fR] [\fIpath\fR]
Reverse a claim: revert the SELinux label, drop both registries, and \(em behind
its own confirm \(em revoke the agent's access, handing the tree back to your own
group (or a named user's group) with the agent's write removed. The directory
stays on disk.
.IP
The path is classified against the allowlist. A claimed project is unclaimed; a
directory with claimed projects nested under it lists them and, behind one
confirm, unclaims each outermost-first; a path
.I inside
a claimed project is refused, naming the nearest claimed parent; a path the
allowlist does not cover at all needs
.BR \-\-force .
.IP
This normalizes rather than restores.
.B Every
extended ACL is cleared (including any predating the claim), the group owner
changes, group write is removed, and directory setgid is cleared \(em so a path the
claim opened lands on 640, or 750 where the owner has execute. World access, which
the claim removed, is not restored: a 644 or 664 file was already 660 while claimed
and ends 640. An owner-only path (0600 or 0700) is untouched at both ends, since the
claim leaves it out of the agent's reach to begin with. The previous permissions are
recorded nowhere, so the operation is
.BR "not reversible" .
Back up first.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-entry
Park the
.B allowed\-projects
line instead of deleting it: the files are handed back exactly as they otherwise would be, and
the entry is left in place with a
.B !
in front of it, as
.B \-\-project\-disable
writes it. For an operator whose allowed-projects is an ordered, commented document and who
unclaims between development stages \(em to hand a tree back with clean permissions before a
release, then claim it again for the next one \(em this keeps the project's line where it was,
comment and all, so the later
.B \-\-project\-claim
restores it in place rather than appending a new line at the end.
.IP
Refused with
.BR \-\-force ,
which unclaims a tree the allowlist does not name, so there is no line to keep. Refused also for a
project nested inside another claimed project, for the reason
.B \-\-project\-disable
gives.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-project\-remove \fR[\fB\-y\fR] [\fIpath\fR]
Release a project
.I and
delete its directory (default: the current directory). Use
.B \-\-project\-unclaim
to release one and keep the files.
.IP
The authorization is an
.I exact
.B allowed\-projects
entry alone: there is no
.BR \-\-force ,
an ancestor or a path inside a project is refused, and so is a project that
contains another claimed project \(em deleting it would take that one with it and
leave it registered at a path that no longer exists. A read-only pre-flight refuses
up front if any directory is not deletable by the acting owner, so a removal never
stops partway through a tree. Teardown removes the SELinux label and both
registries before deleting, so a failed deletion leaves an unregistered tree rather
than a half-deleted one.
.IP
It confirms twice \(em a default-NO prompt, then a typed-name challenge \(em and
neither is answered by a run with no terminal, so a delete needs an operator at the prompt
without
.BR \-y ,
which additionally requires an explicit
.IR path .
.TP
.B \-\-project\-disable \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Park a claimed project (default: the current directory): its
.B allowed\-projects
line is prefixed with
.BR ! ,
which the launch gate reads as an exclusion, so no session starts there until it is
re-enabled. This is the same edit an operator makes by hand, and it edits the same
line \(em in place, keeping its position and any comment \(em so the two workflows
agree and a park-and-restore round trip leaves the file as it was.
.IP
It is a registry change and
.I only
a registry change: the tree keeps its group, its ACLs, its setgid bits and its
SELinux label, so no step has to be re-applied when it comes back. What stops
along with the launches is every root helper that resolves a path's owner through
the allowlist \(em so while a project is disabled the ownership handback does
.I not
restore files written under it, and
.BR \-\-lockdown ", " \-\-reclaim " and " \-\-project\-unclaim
decline to act on it. Disabling a project a session is still writing to therefore
leaves that session's files owned by the sandbox account; stop the session first, or
re-enable and
.B \-\-reclaim
afterwards.
.IP
A path the allowlist does not name is refused rather than parked: there is no entry
to disable, and inventing one would register a project without claiming it. A
project already disabled succeeds and says so.
.IP
A project
.I nested
inside another claimed project is also refused. The
.B !
line it would write is indistinguishable from an exclusion withholding a subtree from the project
above it, and no field in the file could tell the two apart afterwards \(em so re-enabling it later
could only be a guess, on an edit that widens what the agent reaches. The refusal names the two
ways to get the intended effect: unclaim the nested project, or park the one above it. Editing the
line by hand is unaffected; the tool simply will not write that one for you.
.TP
.B \-\-project\-enable \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Restore a project disabled earlier (default: the current directory): the
.B !
is deleted from its line, in place. Sessions may start there again.
.IP
It does not grant any access that was not already granted \(em the tree's permissions never
changed \(em so it does not run a secret scan and does not prompt. A path the allowlist does
not name is refused and points at
.BR \-\-project\-claim :
claiming scans for secrets before granting an agent access to a tree, which is a
different decision from lifting a
.B !
the operator put there. A project already enabled succeeds and says so.
.IP
An exclusion
.I inside
a claimed project is a carve-out \(em a subtree its operator withheld from the agent \(em not a
disabled project, and is refused for that reason: lifting it would hand that subtree over. No verb
writes such a line (see
.BR \-\-project\-disable ),
so one in the file was written by hand and is edited by hand.
.IP
Where the project's own entry is clean but something else still parks it \(em an exclusion on an
ancestor, or a glob that matches \(em the command says so and shows the line responsible, rather
than reporting a project enabled that no session can enter.
.IP
.B \-\-project\-claim
on a disabled project neither claims over it nor duplicates its entry: it reports
the exclusion, shows the line, and offers this same re-enable behind a default-NO
confirm.
.TP
.B \-\-sandbox\-create \fR[\fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-from\fR \fIref\fR] [\fB\-\-branch\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-dir\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Shallow-clone the repository at
.I path
into the sandbox area so the agent never reads the origin's full git history.
Each input has a default and a flag, so the command is scriptable; omit a flag and
it is prompted interactively (or takes its default with no terminal). The exact
.BR "git branch" ", " "git push" ", and " "git clone"
commands are shown for confirmation before any of them run.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-from \fIref\fR
Branch or ref to fork the sandbox branch from (default: the current branch). May be a
local branch, a
.IR <remote>/<ref> ,
or any commit-ish, so a hotfix can be based on
.I main
while you sit on another branch without checking it out.
.TP
.B \-\-branch \fIname\fR
The full sandbox branch to create and track. Any valid git ref is accepted (a flat
name, a
.IR hotfix/x ,
the old
.I ai\-tools/sandbox\-<label>/<leaf>
form, etc.); it is validated with
.BR git\-check\-ref\-format (1),
never rewritten. Default:
.IR sandbox/ <leaf>,
where <leaf> is the last component of
.BR \-\-from .
The default does not carry any host, machine, or operator identity.
.TP
.B \-\-dir \fIname\fR
Directory name for the clone under the sandbox area (default: the repository's base name).
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the final \(lqCreate the sandbox clone?\(rq confirmation. The secret-lockdown and
.I .git
gates still apply.
.RE
.IP
The clone is born owner-only; tip-commit secrets are locked down first, and only past
that gate is the clone opened to the agent and registered. A declined or failed lockdown
stops fail-closed \(em the clone stays private and unregistered; re-run this command on
the clone path to resume securing it.
.TP
.B \-\-sandbox\-push \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Push the sandbox clone's commits to its dedicated remote branch
.RB ( ai\-tools/sandbox\- <user> / <leaf> ).
.TP
.B \-\-sandbox\-remove \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Delete a sandbox clone and unregister it, warning about unpushed commits
first. The remote branch stays for others to merge.
.TP
.B \-\-lockdown \fR[\fIpath\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-y\fR]
Lock secret-named files under the project (\fB.env\fR, \fB*.key\fR, ...) to
owner-only, revoking the agent's read access. Runs via
.BR sudo .
.TP
.B \-\-reclaim \fR[\fB\-\-full\fR] [\fIpath\fR]
Take back ownership of the files the agent wrote under the project \(em including
the
.B .git
tree the per-session sweeps skip \(em without unclaiming it: the project stays
registered and the agent keeps its access. This is the on-demand form of the
ownership handback (run it before an ACL-unaware backup, so ownership \(em not the
ACL alone \(em carries your access into the copy). Runs via
.BR sudo .
.TP
.B \-\-stop
Terminate every running agent session and everything it spawned. This is the one control
that acts on a session
.BR "already running" :
every other operator control \(em unclaim, disabling a provider, revoking an operator \(em
changes only what the
.B next
launch gets. Runs via
.BR sudo :
a session is a transient unit in the sandbox account's own per-user
.BR systemd (1)
manager, and signalling it is root's to do. The bare command needs
.B no password
\(em the operators group holds a
.B NOPASSWD
rule for exactly that form, so a detector with no human present can reach it \(em while
.B \-\-force
and
.B \-\-dry\-run
fall outside the rule and prompt. Why it is granted this way:
.IR /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/session\-stop.md .
.IP
It is
.B not
the way to finish a session you have done with. Use
.B /exit
inside the session for that, which lets it run its own session-end handback. This command
kills the process tree, so no handback runs and the last turn's writes may still be
sandbox-owned: it names the
.B \-\-reclaim
to run for each project it terminated.
.IP
It takes
.B no path
and no authorization input, and there is deliberately no per-project form: every way to
attribute a session to a project is written by the account being stopped, so attribution
is reported to you and never used to decide what a stop reaches. What is terminated is
decided by one fact a session cannot influence: membership of the account's
.B cgroup
slice. A path argument is refused (exit 2) rather than ignored; why that direction, and
what a targeted form would need first, is in
.IR /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/session\-stop.md .
.IP
Sessions are found by
.BR cgroup ,
not by process tree, so a child that called
.BR setsid (2)
or double-forked is still terminated. Each gets 10 seconds to exit on
.B SIGTERM
before it is killed.
.B Nothing is exempt
\(em not the account's own
.BR systemd (1)
manager, whose
.I init.scope
is terminated with everything else, because an exemption is a cgroup a session could move
into. The manager is restarted afterwards, as a separate step reported on its own; if that
fails the command says so and names the fix, and the next launch needs it before it can
start a session. Success means every session that existed at enumeration was terminated
and verified gone from its
.BR cgroup ;
it does not mean none can start afterwards, and it does not describe the manager.
.IP
The report counts agent sessions apart from the account's own plumbing \(em its user manager, its
dbus broker, and a login session scope per
.BR sudo (8)
that crossed pam_systemd. All are terminated; the plumbing is listed after the sessions and marked
.IR "(account plumbing)" ,
so the headline does not report four units of scaffolding as four agent sessions. The split is
presentational \(em it labels and orders rows and decides which get a reclaim command, without
selecting any target. Because the restarted manager is itself inside the swept slice, running the command twice
in a row is not silent: the second run reports zero agent sessions, then terminates and restarts the
manager again. It is idempotent in end state, not in what it prints.
.IP
Exit status carries the outcome:
.B 1
something survived
.BR SIGKILL ,
.B 2
usage \(em an unknown option, or a path,
.B 4
declined at the confirmation,
.B 5
the helper could not run. The request, the answer to the confirmation, each session ended
and which pass ended it are all recorded in
.I /var/log/ai\-tools/stop.log
and journald. What each outcome means, the kernel invariants the stop rests on, and what it
cannot undo:
.IR /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/session\-stop.md .
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Accepted and inert. Every run already terminates every agent session, so this flag exists
only so that a script spelling the intent out is not refused for being explicit.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Skip the grace period and kill immediately. The current turn's unsaved work is lost, and
the trail records that no process was given the chance to flush anything.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-providers
List the installed agents and toolchain integrations, which of them a session
gets, and why: an exact allowlist named in
.IR /etc/ai-tools/operator.conf ,
or each provider's own
.B default_enable
when that file names none. Read-only, and it reports any input the resolver
refused as untrusted. On an SELinux host it also reports the loaded optional
policy groups, and warns with the
.B ai-tools-admin selinux groups enable tmpmap
fix when the
.B dotnet
integration is enabled under Enforcing but the
.B tmpmap
group that its build needs is not loaded.
.TP
.B \-\-audit \fR[\fB\-\-since\fI when\fR]
Report what has refused, been rejected, been stranded, or been flagged since a given
time \(em the detections the sandbox already records but that no command reads
back. Needs
.BR sudo :
the trail it reads is
.I /var/log/ai\-tools/*.log
at
.BR "700 root:root" ,
which the sandbox account can neither read nor append to, so those lines are evidence.
Launch refusals are reported in a section of their own, because
.B ai\-tools\-run
runs as the sandbox account and can reach only journald \(em they are the session's own
account of itself, to reconcile against the trail above rather than to rely on alone.
Exits non-zero when anything is reported, so it can be run unattended from a cron job
or a login banner without parsing its output.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Report ai-tools health: the installed version, whether the toolchain is provisioned,
each managed systemd unit \(em the handback socket, the relabel watcher, and the
toolchain auto-update timer and update service \(em as OK, SKIPPED, DOWN, FAILED,
STALE, or not-installed \(em then, per agent whose package declares one, whether its
entrypoint carries a verified checksum. Read-only. Exits non-zero when anything is reported broken,
so it can be run unattended from a monitor or a cron job. SKIPPED is not broken: it
reports a run that correctly declined to act, such as an update that could not reach the
package registry, and turns STALE if the condition persists.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List registered projects, real and sandbox.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the installed version.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the command summary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
For
.BR \-\-project\-claim :
pre-answer only the proceed prompt; the secret-lockdown, git-history, and
ancestor-traversal questions still ask. For
.BR \-\-lockdown :
skip the confirmation.
For
.BR \-\-project\-unclaim :
pre-answer the confirm. It never answers the hand-back or skip-listed questions.
For
.BR \-\-project\-remove :
pre-answer
.I both
the confirmation and the typed-name challenge \(em the only thing that does, since
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSUME_YES
never answers a default-NO question and the challenge has no default at all. It
then requires an explicit
.IR path ,
so an unattended removal cannot inherit the current directory.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
For
.BR \-\-lockdown :
report what would be locked, and what would be stripped from the paths you have
already sealed by mode, without changing anything. For
.BR "\-\-project\-unclaim \-\-force" :
list every path that would change, with ownership and mode, and apply none of them.
A registered project's unclaim previews itself, so this pairs with
.B \-\-force
only.
.TP
.B \-\-since \fIwhen\fR
For
.BR \-\-audit :
the start of the reporting window \(em anything
.BR date (1)
parses, such as
.RB \(oq "2 days ago" \(cq,
.RB \(oq yesterday \(cq
or
.RB \(oq 2026\-08\-01 \(cq.
Defaults to 7 days ago. A value
.BR date (1)
cannot parse is refused rather than treated as \(lqeverything\(rq, so a typo does not
silently become a reassuring wall of old findings.
.TP
.B \-\-full
For
.BR \-\-reclaim :
also reclaim the heavy skipped trees
.RB ( node_modules ", " .venv ,
\&...), not just the work tree and
.BR .git .
For
.BR \-\-project\-unclaim :
the same trees, where ai-tools ownership survives a copy exactly as it does
elsewhere. The pass reports what it finds there and confirms before including it.
.TP
.B \-\-force
For
.BR \-\-project\-unclaim :
act on a tree the allowlist does
.B not
cover \(em a claimed project copied or moved elsewhere and never unclaimed. It
replaces the allowlist gate with a per-path one: a path is touched only while it
still carries ai-tools ownership, group, or an ai-tools ACL entry, so running it
on a directory that was never claimed leaves it untouched. It does
.B not
relax the protected-paths backstop, the owner guard, the hardlink guard, or the
secret and exclusion skips, and it is refused on a registered project.
.TP
.BI \-\-group " group"
For
.BR \-\-project\-unclaim :
hand the tree to
.I group
without prompting, in both the normal and
.B \-\-force
modes. Without it the command asks whether to hand back and whose group to use,
and a run with no terminal takes the invoking user's group by default \(em so a
script should name the group outright.
.TP
.BI \-\-for " operator"
Act on
.IR operator 's
projects instead of your own. The allowlist entry lands in
.IR operator 's
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
rather than yours, so the project is granted to
.IR operator ,
files the agent writes are handed back to
.IR operator ,
and
.IR operator 's
own agent launches there without further setup.
.IP
This exists for a service account that runs a coding agent but has no password,
and so cannot authenticate the root helpers a claim of its own would invoke: a
human operator performs the claim on its behalf, once, and the account never
meets a password prompt.
.I operator
must already be enrolled
.RB ( "sudo ai\-tools\-admin operators add " \fIoperator\fR ),
since the ownership helpers resolve a project's owner from the
.B OPERATORS
list.
.IP
.B \-\-project\-create
and
.B \-\-project\-remove
write the filesystem
.I as
.IR operator ,
so they additionally need a
.BR sudo (8)
grant to run as that account \(em a separate question from the ai-tools helper
grants, and one a host can answer differently. It is checked before the run
starts rather than partway through.
.IP
Accepted on
.BR \-\-project\-claim ", " \-\-project\-create ", " \-\-project\-unclaim ,
.BR \-\-project\-remove ", " \-\-lockdown ", " \-\-reclaim ", and " \-\-list ;
refused elsewhere rather than ignored. It cannot be combined with
.BR "\-\-project\-unclaim \-\-force" ,
whose tree has no allowlist entry naming an owner and is therefore bound to
the invoking operator.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed.
A dry run that printed its plan, and an idempotent verb that found no work left
to do, both land here.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed.
For
.B \-\-stop
this is the specific case that a process was still running after the kill:
re-run it. For
.B \-\-project\-remove
it also covers a removal that left paths behind: the project is already
unregistered by then, so what remains is out of the agent's reach and can be
deleted by hand.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was rejected.
.B \-\-stop
returns this for a
.I path
argument, which it refuses rather than accepts and ignores.
.TP
.B 3
The target is a protected path \(em a system directory or a home root \(em and
every elevated helper refuses it, whatever the allowlist says.
.TP
.B 5
.B \-\-stop
could not run at all: it was not invoked as root, the sandbox account has no
uid, or the host has no cgroup v2 hierarchy.
No session was signalled.
.TP
.B 130
.B \-\-stop
was interrupted by a signal.
Some sessions may be partially stopped; re-run it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSUME_YES
Set to
.B 1
to answer every question whose default is already
.I yes
without prompting, for unattended runs.
A question defaulting to
.I no
still prompts, so this never turns a refusal into consent.
.PP
Operator-wide settings are not environment variables: they live in
.BR operator.conf (5),
which is root-owned so that a session cannot widen what it is given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
Per-operator allowlist of project paths; a
.RB \(oq ! \(cq-prefixed
line excludes a subtree. Gates where sessions launch \(em not a read boundary.
.TP
.I /etc/ai-tools/operator.conf
Operator registry and skip-list overrides.
.TP
.I /var/opt/ai-tools/sandbox-projects/
The sandbox-clone area;
.I /var/opt/ai-tools/README.md
documents the push-only clone workflow.
.SH EXAMPLES
Claim the project in the current directory, answering each prompt:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-project\-claim
.EE
.RE
.PP
Claim a specific tree, taking every default:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-project\-claim \-y ~/src/myproject
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start a new project: create the directory, initialize git, and claim it. The parent
must already exist, and the directory must not:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-project\-create ~/src/newproject
.EE
.RE
.PP
Release a project and delete its directory. It confirms, then asks you to type the
project's name; the files are not recoverable afterwards:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-project\-remove ~/src/oldproject
.EE
.RE
.PP
List what is registered, and what the sandbox account resolves for a session:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-list
ai\-tools \-\-providers
.EE
.RE
.PP
End every running agent session on the host (no password; run it as yourself):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-stop
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a project back and leave the directory on disk:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-project\-unclaim ~/src/myproject
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai\-tools\-admin (8),
.BR operator.conf (5),
.BR setfacl (1),
.BR sudo (8).
.PP
The lifecycle guide \(em claim against sandbox clone, what each prompt grants,
and the recovery paths:
.I /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/project\-lifecycle.md
.PP
Stopping a running session \(em what each outcome means, the invariants the stop
rests on, and what a stop cannot undo:
.I /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/session\-stop.md
